<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isle of Wight</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Isle of Wight is an island in the English Channel off the coast of Hampshire, known to the Romans as Vectis.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isma'il ibn Ahmad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Isma'il ibn Ahmad, known as Ismail Samani, was the Samanid emir who made his family the rulers of Transoxiana and Khurasan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isma'il Pasha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Isma'il Pasha was a grandson of Muhammad Ali who ruled Egypt from 1863 and received the title khedive from the Ottoman sultan in 1867.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismailism"><span class="enc-name">Isma'ilism</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Isma'ilism is a branch of Shia Islam that recognises Ismail, son of the imam Ja'far al-Sadiq, and his descendants as the line of imams.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-abd-al-nabi"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Abd al-Nabi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Abd al-Nabi was a Masalit holy man who built up authority among the Masalit people on the western fringe of the Darfur Sultanate in the 1870s, as Darfur fell to…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-adil-shah"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Adil Shah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Adil Shah was the second Adil Shahi sultan of Bijapur, succeeding his father Yusuf Adil Shah in 1510 and ruling until 1534.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-al-jazari"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-al-jazari.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ismail al-Jazari</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail al-Jazari was an engineer and inventor who served the Artuqid rulers of Diyarbakır in upper Mesopotamia for about 25 years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-al-zafir"><span class="enc-name">Ismail al-Zafir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail al-Zafir, of the Berber family of the Banu Dhi al-Nun, was the first ruler of the dynasty in the taifa of Toledo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-bey-of-candar"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Bey of Candar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Bey was the last independent ruler of the Candarid beylik of Kastamonu and Sinop in northern Anatolia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-bey-of-the-nogai-horde"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Bey of the Nogai Horde</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Bey was a ruler of the Nogai Horde, the nomadic confederation of the Volga steppe.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ismail I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail I was the founder of the Safavid dynasty, who took Tabriz and was proclaimed shah in 1501.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-ii-of-granada"><span class="enc-name">Ismail II of Granada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail II was Nasrid emir of Granada from August 1359 to June 1360.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ismail III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail III was a young Safavid prince, Abu Torab, set on the throne of Iran in 1750 by Ali Mardan Khan Bakhtiari and Karim Khan Zand to lend their rule legitimacy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-kamil-pasha"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Kamil Pasha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Kamil Pasha was a son of Muhammad Ali of Egypt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-khan-of-shaki"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Khan of Shaki</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Khan was the last khan of Shaki, a khanate in the north of present-day Azerbaijan, which had accepted Russian protection in 1805.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-khan-qashqai"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Khan Qashqai</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Khan Qashqai, titled Solat al-Dowleh, was ilkhan of the Qashqai tribal confederation in southern Iran.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-omar-guelleh"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Omar Guelleh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Omar Guelleh is a Djiboutian politician who has been president of Djibouti since 1999, succeeding his uncle Hassan Gouled Aptidon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismail-qemali"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ismail-qemali.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ismail Qemali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ismail Qemali was an Albanian statesman from Vlorë who had served as an Ottoman official and liberal deputy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">İsmet İnönü</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">İsmet İnönü was a Turkish general and statesman, Mustafa Kemal Atatürk's closest ally.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/isny-im-allgau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/isny-im-allgau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isny im Allgäu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Isny im Allgäu is a town in the western Allgäu in Baden-Württemberg that grew around a Benedictine abbey founded in 1096.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/isocrates"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/isocrates.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isocrates</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Isocrates was an Athenian orator and teacher of rhetoric.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/isokelekel"><span class="enc-name">Isokelekel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Isokelekel is a semi-legendary hero of Pohnpei in Micronesia who, according to oral tradition, overthrew the Saudeleur dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/isonokami-shrine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/isonokami-shrine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isonokami Shrine</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Isonokami Shrine is a Shinto shrine in Tenri, Nara Prefecture, one of the oldest in Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/isparta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/isparta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isparta</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Isparta is a city in the Lakes Region of south-western Turkey, capital of the province of the same name.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ispir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ispir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">İspir</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">İspir is a town in Erzurum province in north-eastern Turkey, on the upper Çoruh river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/isputahsu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/isputahsu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Isputahsu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Isputahsu was a king of Kizzuwatna in south-eastern Anatolia around the 15th century BCE and one of the first known rulers of that kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Israel Finkelstein</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Israel Finkelstein is an Israeli archaeologist at Tel Aviv University and a long-time co-director of the excavations at Megiddo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/israelites"><span class="enc-name">Israelites</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Israelites were an ancient Semitic people of the Levant, ancestors of the Jewish people, who formed the kingdoms of Israel and Judah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/issa-clan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/issa-clan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Issa (clan)</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Issa are a Somali clan of the Dir family, living in Djibouti, the Somali Region of Ethiopia and north-western Somalia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/issa-dalmatia"><span class="enc-name">Issa (Dalmatia)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Issa was an ancient Greek city on the island of Vis in the central Adriatic, now in Croatia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kurgan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/issyk-kurgan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Issyk kurgan</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Issyk kurgan is a Saka burial mound near the town of Esik, about 50 kilometres east of Almaty in Kazakhstan, excavated in 1969–1970.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/issyk-kul"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/issyk-kul.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Issyk-Kul</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Issyk-Kul is a large mountain lake without an outlet in north-eastern Kyrgyzstan, lying in the Tian Shan at about 1,600 metres above sea level.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/istakhr"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/istakhr.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Istakhr</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Istakhr was an ancient city in Fars, south-western Iran, a few kilometres north of Persepolis.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><