ss="enc-name">Ius regale montanorum</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ius regale montanorum was a mining code issued around 1300 by Wenceslaus II, king of Bohemia, for the silver mines of Kutná Hora.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iuvavum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/iuvavum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iuvavum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Iuvavum was a Roman town in the province of Noricum on the Salzach, on the site of modern Salzburg in Austria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivailo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivailo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivailo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivailo was a swineherd who led a peasant rebellion in north-eastern Bulgaria in 1277 against Mongol raids and the failures of the nobility.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Alexander was tsar of the Second Bulgarian Empire from 1331 to 1371.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-andreyevich-of-mozhaysk"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Andreyevich of Mozhaysk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Andreyevich was prince of Mozhaysk, an appanage of the Grand Duchy of Moscow.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Asen I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Asen I was the co-founder and first ruler of the Second Bulgarian Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Asen II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Asen II, son of Ivan Asen I, was tsar of Bulgaria from 1218 to 1241.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-chistyakov"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-chistyakov.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Chistyakov</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Chistyakov (1900–1979) was a Soviet colonel general who commanded armies at Stalingrad and Kursk during the Second World War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-crnojevic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-crnojevic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Crnojević</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Crnojević was lord of Zeta, the forerunner of Montenegro, from 1465 to 1490.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-dmitrievich-of-pereslavl"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Dmitrievich of Pereslavl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Dmitrievich was the last prince of Pereslavl-Zalessky, a son of Grand Prince Dmitry of Vladimir and grandson of Alexander Nevsky.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-fyodorov"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Fyodorov</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Fyodorov was a printer who, with Pyotr Mstislavets, produced the Apostol of 1564, the first precisely dated book printed in Moscow.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Gundulić</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Gundulić was a poet of the Republic of Ragusa (Dubrovnik) and the leading figure of Croatian Baroque literature.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan I of Moscow</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan I Danilovich, called Kalita ('moneybag'), was Prince of Moscow from 1325 and Grand Prince of Vladimir from 1328 until his death in 1340.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan III was Grand Prince of Moscow in the late fifteenth century, often called Ivan the Great.</span></a></li></ul>
